@@s001 Code: topological sort in Java
@@s002 Code: topological sort in Java
@@s003 Kahn's method: incoming degrees, a queue of sources, and cycle detection when not all the nodes get written.
@@s004 Incoming degrees and queue
@@s005 The edges are directed: <code>adj.get(u)</code> contains the successors of <code>u</code>, and <code>entrante[v]</code> counts how many edges enter <code>v</code>.
@@s006 Incoming degrees and queue
@@s007 An array of integers: Java initialises it all to 0. It is the incoming degree of each node.
@@s008 Each edge <code>u → v</code> increases the incoming degree of <code>v</code> by 1.
@@s009 The sources, that is the nodes without incoming edges, enter the queue at once.
@@s010 Syntax notes
@@s011 <code>int[]</code> has a fixed length and default values 0 (for <code>boolean</code> it would be <code>false</code>).
@@s012 <code>==</code> compares the values of primitives; <code>.equals</code> is used for objects such as <code>String</code>.
@@s013 Removing the nodes
@@s014 Each extracted node is written in the order, and its outgoing edges are deleted by lowering the degree of the successors.
@@s015 Removing the nodes
@@s016 I extract a source: I write it in the order.
@@s017 <code>--</code> decrements: the edge <code>u → v</code> has been removed.
@@s018 If <code>v</code> has no incoming edges left, it is ready.
@@s019 Syntax notes
@@s020 <code>List&lt;Integer&gt; ordine = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;()</code>: <code>ordine.get(i)</code> reads an element, <code>.size()</code> counts.
@@s021 Detecting the cycle
@@s022 If the graph has a cycle, the nodes of the cycle never reach degree 0: they stay unwritten. So counting is enough.
@@s023 Detecting the cycle
@@s024 Fewer nodes written than total nodes: the graph has a cycle and an order does not exist.
@@s025 Syntax notes
@@s026 <code>println</code> starts a new line; <code>print</code> does not. Here I use <code>print</code> on the output built.
@@s027 Running it
@@s028 Example of input and output (actually compiled and run).
